@* A self-contained "How to read the Bible" helper, shown on the Bible home and every book and chapter page.
   It explains how the canon is arranged, how references read, the two translations, and the older book names. *@

<FloatingGuide OrbKey="@OrbLauncher.Bible" HideTrigger="HideTrigger" Slot="Slot" Title="How to read the Bible" Eyebrow="A short orientation" AriaLabel="How to read the Bible">
    <Icon>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="5 3 22 26" fill="currentColor"
             xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <path d="M 9 4 C 7.355469 4 6 5.355469 6 7 L 6 25 C 6 26.644531 7.355469 28 9 28 L 26 28 L 26 4 Z M 9 6 L 24 6 L 24 22 L 9 22 C 8.648438 22 8.316406 22.074219 8 22.1875 L 8 7 C 8 6.433594 8.433594 6 9 6 Z M 15 8 L 15 11 L 12 11 L 12 13 L 15 13 L 15 20 L 17 20 L 17 13 L 20 13 L 20 11 L 17 11 L 17 8 Z M 9 24 L 24 24 L 24 26 L 9 26 C 8.433594 26 8 25.566406 8 25 C 8 24.433594 8.433594 24 9 24 Z" />
        </svg>
    </Icon>
    <ChildContent>
        <p>
            The Bible is one book with many human authors and one divine Author. The Church, guided by the Holy Spirit,
            discerned which books belong to it, and she reads them together with Sacred Tradition and under the teaching of the Magisterium
            (<em>Dei Verbum</em> 9-10).
        </p>

        <GuideSection Title="The shape of the whole work">
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Old Testament">46 books: the Pentateuch (the five books of Moses), the historical books, the wisdom books, and the prophets.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="New Testament">27 books: the four Gospels, the Acts of the Apostles, the epistles of St. Paul, the Catholic epistles, and the Apocalypse.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Deuterocanonical books">
                    Tobias, Judith, Wisdom, Ecclesiasticus (Sirach), Baruch, 1 and 2 Machabees, and the Greek portions of Esther and Daniel.
                    The Church has always received them as Scripture; Protestant Bibles set them aside in the sixteenth century, which is why they are shorter.
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Appendix">a few texts printed after the Vulgate for reference (such as the Prayer of Manasses); they are not part of the canon.</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Reading a reference">
            <p>A reference names the book, then the chapter, then the verse:</p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="John 3:16">the Gospel of St. John, chapter 3, verse 16.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Matthew 5:3-12">chapter 5, verses 3 through 12.</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            <p>
                The sacred writers did not number anything. Chapters were added by Stephen Langton in the thirteenth century,
                and verses by Robert Estienne in 1551, so a chapter break can fall in the middle of a thought.
            </p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Two translations">
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Douay-Rheims">
                    the classic English Catholic Bible, translated from the Latin Vulgate by English exiles (1582-1610) and revised by Bishop Challoner in the eighteenth century.
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="CPDV">
                    the Catholic Public Domain Version, a modern English rendering of the same Vulgate. It is easier to read, but it is not an official Church translation.
                </GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Old names">
            <p>Because both follow the Vulgate, some books carry older names:</p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="1-2 Kings">1-2 Samuel</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="3-4 Kings">1-2 Kings</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="1-2 Paralipomenon">1-2 Chronicles</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="1-2 Esdras">Ezra and Nehemiah</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Abdias">Obadiah</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Aggeus">Haggai</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Machabees">Maccabees</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Apocalypse">Revelation</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
        </GuideSection>

        <PsalmNumberingNote>
            <p>
                Both translations number the Psalms by the traditional scheme of the Greek Septuagint and the Latin Vulgate,
                which the Church used for centuries. Modern Catholic Bibles (such as the NABRE and RSV-CE) and the English Liturgy of the Hours
                follow the Hebrew numbering instead, so the same psalm may carry two numbers.
            </p>
        </PsalmNumberingNote>
    </ChildContent>

    <Footer>
        <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="context.CloseAsync">Begin reading</button>
    </Footer>
</FloatingGuide>

@code {
    [Parameter] public bool HideTrigger { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public int Slot { get; set; }
}
